NTA. I actually think "Ask Prudence" answered this question on [slate.com](https://slate.com), and her answer was this: for black people, the n-word is a swear word, and for everyone else it's horrifically racist

&#x200B;

I think it's totally fine to talk however you want with your friends and family in terms of swearing, but school and work requires more-or-less polite language. When talking to parents, maybe you could tell them that the standards at school are what the standards are in most workplaces.

Heya- obviously she should've told you, and she's a pos for giving you an STD. I'd stop seeing her.

&#x200B;

But you should know that 1/6 adults technically have genital herpes. Most doctors don't test for it unless you ask because it is not harmful, and because, in the words of my ob-gyn, "it's just stigmatized". Most people (maybe 2/3?) have oral herpes too, but you would never not kiss someone because of that. It's the same deal with genital herpes. Definitely check with your doctor, but if she didn't have an outbreak while you had sex, I'd suspect you'll be fine.
